# Coldwire

A mountable Rails engine serving a Cache API service worker for Hotwire Native apps.
Tagline: *When your Hotwire wires go cold.*

Keep the implementation small and aligned with these two sources:

- Cache API: [https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Cache](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Cache)
- Joe Masilotti: [https://newsletter.masilotti.com/p/offline-mode-for-hotwire-native-apps](https://newsletter.masilotti.com/p/offline-mode-for-hotwire-native-apps)

## Fetch handler

Store successful GETs with `put(timestampedRequest(request), response.clone())`; read with
`match(request, MATCH_OPTIONS)`.

- **HTML is network-first.** Pages recache on every view so the timestamp moves and the copy
  stays fresh, falling back to the cached entry when the network fails.
- **Assets are cache-first.** Recaching every stylesheet on a visit would bury the page in the
  inspector's recent list.
- Guard writes with `isCacheable(request, response)`. `put()` rejects error statuses and 206,
  but it does **not** reject a followed redirect — that has to be checked explicitly, and the
  omission is what broke signed-out offline launches. A signed-out `/` follows a 302 to the
  sign-in page and arrives `ok: true, status: 200, redirected: true`; storing it puts the
  sign-in body under the `/` key, and serving a redirected response for a navigation is a
  network error by spec. Skip those rather than synthesizing a new Response.

Precache, force-offline, and the inspector all use this same `match` / `put(clone)` path.

## Cache API constraints

- Read with `match(request, { ignoreVary: true })`. Rails answers HTML with `Vary: Accept`, and
  `match()` honors Vary by default; precaching fetches with `Accept: */*` while Turbo Drive and
  Turbo Frame requests send `Accept: text/html, application/xhtml+xml`. Without `ignoreVary` a
  precached page only ever matches another precache, so offline falls through to the offline
  page while on-demand caching still appears to work.
- Items do not update or expire unless the script does it. The Cache API does not honor HTTP
  freshness headers (`Date`, `Expires`, `Cache-Control`), and WebKit drops `Date` on `match()`.
  The timestamp lives on the stored *request key* (`TIMESTAMP_HEADER`, unix seconds) — `keys()`
  returns it and URL matching still finds the entry. Do not reach for a separate side-channel
  store.
- `ignore_query_params` (default on) sets `ignoreSearch` in `MATCH_OPTIONS` **and** strips the
  search in `cacheKey()`. Both halves are needed: matching alone still writes one entry per
  distinct query string, and a map rewriting lat/lng/zoom per pan would bury the cache. Known
  blunt edge, to revisit — it also collapses `/search?q=` style URLs onto one entry.
- Size for the inspector is `response.clone().blob().size`.
- `keys()` + `match(request)` to list; `caches.delete(name)` to clear.
- Same-origin GET only. Never intercept Range requests (map tiles, media), or anything under
  `Coldwire.excluded_paths`.

## Offline fallback

- Serve it with **status 200**, not 503. Hotwire Native treats a non-2xx visit as a failed
  request and shows its own native error screen. Its adapter passes only location, identifier,
  and status code to the native side — the body never crosses over, so no iOS-side override can
  rescue an error status.
- Keep `<meta name="turbo-cache-control" content="no-cache">` in the page template so a 200
  offline page is not snapshotted and restored once back online.
- When the request carries a `Turbo-Frame` header, answer with a matching `<turbo-frame id>`.
  A frame discards a response with no matching frame and sits on its loading state forever.
  Escape the id; it comes from a request header.
- The fallback **page** must boot Turbo, or Hotwire Native reports `.load(.notPresent)` —
  "The page could not be loaded because Turbo is not present" — and shows its own screen. Its
  adapter (`turbo.js`) waits for `window.Turbo` and calls `pageLoadFailed()` on timeout. Plain
  HTML is not renderable in the app. Import Turbo **alone** (`offline_entry_point`), never the
  app entry point: a module graph fails to evaluate if any one file is uncached, and then
  Turbo never lands. The fallback **frame** needs none of this — it is a fragment going into a
  page that already has Turbo.
- Both fallbacks carry a retry link, and neither can hardcode a URL — the templates are baked
  when the worker is built. The page uses `href=""` (resolves to the served URL) with
  `data-turbo="false"`, because a Turbo visit to the identical URL can be treated as same-page
  and do nothing. The frame uses the `coldwire:retry-url` token, substituted with
  `request.url` and escaped; an empty href inside a frame resolves to the *page* and would
  load the whole document into the card.
- The fallback page's `[data-turbo-track=reload]` elements must match a real page's, in the
  same order — that is what `offline_head` is for. Turbo refuses to render a page whose
  tracked signature differs and calls `pageInvalidated`; the SDK answers that by showing the
  activity indicator and reloading (`Session#webViewDidInvalidatePage`). Going forward that
  recovers, but on a back navigation it runs mid-pop and the spinner never clears. Because
  `offline_head` pulls the host's stylesheet in, the fallback must state its own typography
  rather than relying on UA defaults a reset would strip.
- The fallback page's CSS goes in the **body**, scoped to `.coldwire-offline`. Turbo's head
  merge copies new `<style>` and `<link rel=stylesheet>` elements in and never removes them —
  only provisional elements (metas, title) are replaced. A `<style>` in the head therefore
  installs itself into the host app for the rest of the session, and a bare `body { padding }`
  rule pushes every later page around until a full reload clears it. In the body it goes with
  the body swap; the scoping means it is inert even if it does outlive the page.
- Only return HTML when the request wants HTML (`mode === "navigate"`, `destination ===
  "document"`, or an `Accept` containing `text/html`). Everything else gets an empty 504 — an
  HTML body just produces a broken asset.

## Engine boundaries

- Nothing in the gem may assume Tailwind or any host CSS framework. The debug page ships plain
  CSS and `coldwire-` class names.
- `ServiceWorkerController` inherits from `ActionController::Base`, **not** the host's
  `ApplicationController`. The worker must be fetchable before sign-in; an authentication
  filter there breaks registration. It also needs `skip_forgery_protection`: Rails guards
  JavaScript responses against cross-origin `<script>` embedding, and the browser's worker
  loader is not an XHR, so that guard rejects legitimate registrations with a 422.
- The engine **is** namespace-isolated, which points bare route helpers inside it at the
  engine's own routes. Two places need the host's routes instead, and both are handled
  explicitly — do not "fix" them by dropping `isolate_namespace`, which removes the
  `coldwire.*` proxy entirely:
  - `Coldwire::ApplicationController` re-exposes `Rails.application.routes.url_helpers` as a
    view helper, so a host layout calling `root_path` still renders.
  - `prefetch_urls` is `instance_exec`'d against `Rails.application.routes.url_helpers`, so a
    host writing `site_path(site)` gets their route and not a 404 inside the mount.
- The worker is served from the mount point, so its default scope is that directory. The
  response sends `Service-Worker-Allowed` and the registration snippet asks for the matching
  scope. Do not assume a root mount.
- Only two of Coldwire's own endpoints are excluded from interception: the worker script and
  the manifest. Caching either would strand the app on a stale copy of the thing meant to
  refresh it. The **debug page is not excluded** — it is ordinary HTML, and excluding the whole
  mount made it unreachable offline, which is exactly when you want to inspect the cache. Hosts
  allowlist it like any other page. Its precache button will fail offline, correctly; the
  inspector, clear, and force-offline controls are client-side and still work.
- Host apps override the offline page, offline frame, and debug page by shadowing the template
  paths. Keep those templates free of request context and current-user helpers: they are
  rendered when the worker script is built and embedded as string literals.

## Authentication

- Cached pages hold whatever the fetching session could see. `cache_identity` is compared in
  `localStorage` on each page load and the cache is dropped when it changes — that is what
  makes sign-out and account switching safe. Keep the comparison on the page, not in the
  worker: a worker global does not survive termination.
- **A missing stored identity is not a changed identity.** `getItem` returns null when nothing
  is stored, and localStorage and the cache store are evicted independently — so treating null
  as "somebody else" destroys a good cache the first time localStorage comes back empty. On a
  cold launch that means opening to the offline page with everything needed sitting in the
  cache. Record it and move on.
- **Never discard the cache while offline.** It is the only thing holding the app up and
  cannot be refilled. Leave the stored identity alone too, so the mismatch survives to be
  acted on once there is a connection.
- Auth paths go in `cache_blocklist` (intercepted, never auto-stored), **not** `excluded_paths`
  (never intercepted). An unintercepted request offline dies as a network error and Hotwire
  Native shows its own screen; the point of intercepting is to reach our offline page.
- The host's cold-boot URL has to be cacheable. A login path is not: signed in it is a 302,
  and redirects are never stored, so an offline launch has nothing to serve. Boot into a real
  page and let the server redirect to login when signed out.
- Anything fetching engine JSON must treat `response.redirected` as "signed out". A followed
  redirect arrives as a 200 of HTML and would otherwise surface as a JSON parse error.

## Offline marker

- `offline_marker` (default on) stamps HTML served from cache *because the network failed* —
  both fallback paths in `handleFetch`, never a plain cache-first asset hit. A page served
  fresh must carry no marker, or "am I stale?" stops meaning anything.
- Stamp **both** `<html>` attributes and a head `<meta>`. The attributes cover the first paint
  of a cold boot so a banner does not flash in; the meta covers Turbo visits, which swap the
  body and merge the head but never copy `<html>` attributes. The helper mirrors meta → html
  on `turbo:load`.
- The timestamp lives on the stored request key, so recover it with
  `cache.keys(request, MATCH_OPTIONS)` — not from the response, which has no usable Date.
- Rewriting the body costs a `text()` read; keep it on the offline path only, and bail early
  for non-HTML and for bodies with no `<html>` tag (frame responses).

## Allowlist and blocklist

- `cache_allowlist` / `cache_blocklist` gate **automatic caching only**. `isCacheable` holds the
  universal rules (GET, ok, not redirected) and is used by both paths; `isAutoCacheable` holds
  the lists and is called only from `handleFetch`. Do not fold them together — `fetchAndCache`
  must stay list-free so the manifest can store anything.
- Empty allowlist means everything is allowed. The blocklist wins over the allowlist.
- Entries are strings (segment-prefix match) or Regexps (tested against the pathname).
  Serialized as `{type, source, flags}` so a Regexp survives JSON, then rebuilt by
  `compileRules`. Only the `i` flag crosses over.
- Validate on assignment, not at render: a bad pattern should fail at boot rather than 500 the
  worker and quietly take caching down. Reject `\A`/`\z`/`\Z` — JavaScript reads them as
  identity escapes, so `\A` becomes a literal "A" and the rule silently never matches — and
  reject the `x` and `m` flags, which mean different things in JS.

## Manifest sync

- There is no background scheduling primitive to build on: WebKit has no Background Sync,
  Periodic Background Sync, or Background Fetch. A page load hands work to the worker and the
  worker runs it independently. Do not reach for `periodicsync` — it will not fire here.
- The throttle stamp lives in `localStorage` on the page, not in the worker: a worker global
  does not survive termination, which is exactly the case this has to withstand.
- **Stamp only on completion.** A sync cut short must leave no stamp so the next page load
  carries on; stamping when the sync *starts* would strand an interrupted run until the whole
  interval elapsed. Resuming needs no bookmark — each pass recomputes what is missing from
  what is in the cache, so an interruption costs only the in-flight requests.
- Progress and completion are broadcast to every client via `clients.matchAll`, not returned
  to the caller. The page that triggered a sync is usually gone by the time it lands, and can
  neither record it nor draw it. One message type (`coldwire:sync`) carrying `state`, so the
  page stamping the clock and the page drawing progress cannot drift apart.
- Only `state: "finished"` with `complete: true` restarts the clock. A batch that stopped at
  the limit is finished but not complete.
- `sync_max_attempts` is the circuit breaker for a manifest that can never finish; without it
  an unfetchable URL means a sync attempt on every navigation, forever.
- One sync at a time (`syncing` promise). The page triggers on `turbo:load` and on `online`.
- A sync fetches manifest URLs that are missing or older than `max_age`, skips the rest, and
  stops at `sync_batch_limit` so a first run on cellular does not pull everything.
- Retiring is limited to entries carrying `MANAGED_HEADER`, set only on manifest URLs
  themselves — never on the assets they reference, which are shared and would thrash. Assets
  and casually-browsed pages must survive a sync untouched.
- Compare URLs through `cacheUrl()` when deciding what is unlisted, or every entry looks
  unlisted as soon as a query string is involved.

## Precache

- Follow subresources only — stylesheets, scripts, images, `srcset`, importmap entries. Never
  follow `<a href>` or this becomes a site crawler.
- Progress streams over the same `MessagePort` as the final result, distinguished by
  `type: "progress"`. The page's timeout re-arms on each tick, so it bounds silence rather than
  total work.
- The manifest (`prefetch_urls`) is the host app's business. Coldwire never guesses it and does
  not render the list in the UI.
